  "query_preview": "..\n\n...WINDY CONDITIONS TODAY...\n\nToday and tonight: A low pressure system and cold front coming \nashore over western Washington and Oregon this morning will \nsweep through the Pacific Northwest and bring rains, high \nmountain snow, a small chance of thunderstorms and windy \nconditions. This low loses definition inland later this \nmorning, but a secondary surface low deepens over southwest \nAlberta. That and high pressure nosing into southwest Washington\nwill increase pressure gradients across the Inland Northwest. \nOverall, expect strong southwest winds around 20-30 mph with \ngusts around 40-50 mph late this morning through this evening. \nWinds will start to relax around sunset and surface based \ninstability will erode as surface inversions form. Chances for \nhigh mountain snow are limited to the Cascades above 5000 feet \nlate this afternoon through tonight.\n\nThe cold front passage this morning and early afternoon will \nbring light rain to much of the region. By sunrise, the band of\nprecipitation will be sweeping through the Columbia Basin. This\nband of precipitation along and ahead of the cold front will \nhave moved through most of the region by 4 PM, leaving a \nconditionally unstable air mass behind it. The 500 mb cold pool \nwill overspread the area in the late morning and early \nafternoon, with temperatures around -19 to -21C. Surface-based \nCAPE will quickly increase to around 200-500 J/kg into the \nafternoon. Showers will form across much of the region, bringing\nthe chance (10-30%) for thunderstorms, particularly near the \nCanadian border. Associated threats would be brief heavy rain \n(minor burn scar threat in the Cascades), gusty winds up to \n50 mph (although background winds will already be strong), \nsmall hail, and isolated to scattered lightning. Strong winds \nfrom thunderstorm outflows and the broader-scale strong winds \nwill bring a risk for blowing dust across the Columbia Basin \nthis afternoon. The HREF 10th to 90th total precipitation for \nMoses Lake through early this afternoon is only 0.04-0.12 \ninches, likely not enough to completely stop blowing dust. \n\nThere is a 40-60% chance of 1 inch of snow in the Enchantments \nof southwest Chelan County and Washington Pass through Saturday \nmorning. Temperatures tonight will be much cooler in the low 30s\nto mid 40s as the post frontal airmass looks dry (PWATs \n50-80%). \n\nSaturday through Thursday: Conditions look precipitation-free \nthrough Monday as the dry airmass over the Inland Northwest\nstays put. High temperatures will be near normal to slightly \nbelow normal in the 60s. Many locations in the northern valleys \nand even parts of the Spokane area to Palouse will see their \nfirst frost or freeze with this pattern, so wrap up those \ngardens. We will keep a close eye on Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning for frost and we may have to consider highlights. Clouds\nincrease Monday evening with the next system. That system will \nbring beneficial rains and high mountain snow primarily to the \nCascades and north Idaho thanks to strong zonal flow. There is a\n40-60% chance of 0.5 inches of rain near the Cascade crest and \na 5-10% chance for the Idaho Panhandle from Tuesday morning to \nWednesday morning. Conditions look cool, but dry after this \nsystem with minor shortwave ridging mid to late week. \n/DB/Solveig",
